From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Subject: [PATCH] phy: allwinner: A733 combo-PHY -- the DisplayPort reset and link-control sequence

`phy-allwinner-a733-combophy-register-a-typec-mux` registered the Type-C mux and stopped there on purpose: entering DP alt mode
printed a warning and left COMB0 in its USB configuration, because writing a
guessed DP setup into a PHY carrying a HW-proven 10 Gbps link is not a trade this
tree makes. This adds the wrapper half of the DP personality -- the LINK_CTRL0
reset staircase, the raw-serdes transmitter enable, and the two PMA handshakes --
derived from the UM and checked against a capture of a working link.

It is the reset and link-control sequence, not a DP driver. What is still missing
is named at the end rather than implied.

WHAT THE END STATE IS, AND HOW IT IS KNOWN
------------------------------------------

The 8 GiB board was run with the vendor 6.6 stack, a ThinkVision T23d-10 on Type-C J4,
DP alt mode active (svid ff01, mode 1), pin assignment C, orientation REVERSE, and
the COMB0 wrapper page was read back through /dev/mem. Four registers describe the
whole wrapper side of that link:

    LINK_CTRL0   0x06C01000 = 0x00001131
    LINK_CTRL3   0x06C0100C = 0x00000011
    LINK_STUS0   0x06C01900 = 0x00000111   (read-only -- the PHY's answer)
    DP_RAWS_TXDW 0x06C06004 = 0x00000FF1

That is the target. This patch's claim is that its sequence produces exactly those
values; it is not a claim that a monitor lights up (see LIMITS).

Field names and offsets come from A733 UM V0.92 chapter 18.2.6; the vendor's combo0_dp_phy_reset()
(bsp/drivers/phy/sunxi-cadence-combophy.c:769-859, the radxa/allwinner-bsp tree) was used
as a hint for the *ordering* only, and every step below is justified against the UM
and the capture, not against it. Where the UM does not name something, this header
says so instead of inventing a name.

STEP BY STEP -- WHICH REGISTER EACH STEP ACCOUNTS FOR
-----------------------------------------------------

1. LINK_CTRL2 0x1008, clear bit1 IDDQ_EN_SEL and bit0 IDDQ_EN_SOFT.
   Meaning: with both bits set the PHY is in IDDQ (power-down); with both clear it
   is powered up. Accounts for: 0x06C01008, which is absent from the capture's
   non-zero list, i.e. reads 0x00000000. Idempotent -- the USB bring-up already
   cleared it -- and reused verbatim from a733_combophy_power_up().

2. LINK_CTRL0 0x1000 written as four ABSOLUTE values, not read-modify-writes:

       0x0000 | dir      everything asserted, plug direction latched
       0x0001 | dir      + bit0 PHY_RESET_N          (UM 18.2.6.5)
       0x0101 | dir      + bit8 DP_LINK_RESET_N_SOFT (UM 18.2.6.5)
       0x0131 | dir      + bit4 PIPE_LINK_RESET_N_SOFT
                         + bit5 PIPE_LINK_RESET_N_USB3P1_MASK

   with 1 ms between each. Accounts for: LINK_CTRL0 = 0x00001131 (dir =
   TYPEC_CONN_DIR = bit12, set here because the capture was taken with a REVERSE
   plug; a normal plug gives 0x00000131). Every bit of the measured value is
   accounted for -- 12, 8, 5, 4, 0 -- with nothing left over.

   Absolute, because that is what the staircase is for: each write both releases
   the next reset and holds the previous ones, and a read-modify-write cannot
   express "everything else off" in the first step. The USB path (`phy-allwinner-a733-add-serdes-combophy`) reaches a
   different value, 0x00000111, by OR-ing bits into whatever was there.

   bit5 is the one bit that separates the two personalities. The USB path never
   sets it. UM 18.2.6.5: bit5 masks the PIPE reset that the USB3P1 controller drives
   into PHY0 (1 = masked); that reset only acts while bit5 is 0 and bit4
   PIPE_LINK_RESET_N_SOFT is 1. So with bit5 set, bit4 becomes the only source of the
   PIPE link reset and the dwc3 core can no longer yank it -- which is precisely
   what is needed while the lanes carry DP and the USB3 controller is still there.
   The capture shows it set; the USB-only silicon reading of the same register
   shows it clear (0x00000111). Two
   states, one differing bit, and the UM explains it.

   One deliberate deviation from the vendor. UM 18.2.6.5 requires bit12 to be
   static while phy0_reset_n is de-asserted. The vendor writes the direction
   *after* the 0x131 step, i.e. after phy0_reset_n is already high. This patch
   folds the direction into all four writes instead, so it is latched while
   phy0_reset_n is still low, and the end value is identical. The vendor's own
   ordering is usually a no-op re-affirmation for it (its set_mode and
   combo0_configure_usb_dp write the direction beforehand), which is probably why
   it never bit -- but the UM is the spec here and the vendor is the hint. If a
   board ever disagrees, the vendor's literal ordering is one line away, and the
   driver comment says so.

3. Poll LINK_STUS0 0x1900 bit0 PMA_CMN_READY, budget 5 ms (vendor: 1000 x 5 us).
   Accounts for: bit0 of LINK_STUS0 = 0x00000111.
   UM 18.2.6.9: bit0 PMA_CMN_READY reports the PMA common block as up, and the
   UM names it as a step of both the Raw SerDes start-up and of power-state
   changes. Raw SerDes is
   the DP personality -- so the UM itself puts this wait inside the DP startup,
   and it belongs BEFORE the transmitters are switched on. The USB path does the
   opposite: `phy-allwinner-a733-add-serdes-combophy` writes DP_RAWS_TXDW_CONV_CTRL first and only checks CMN_READY at
   the very end, as a fallback with a PMA nudge. That ordering is left alone.

4. DP_RAWS_TXDW_CONV_CTRL (top part-2 + 0x0004 = 0x06C06004) = 0x0FF1: bit0,
   [7:4] = 0xF, [11:8] = 0xF. Accounts for: 0x06C06004 = 0x00000FF1 exactly.
   The register summary first used for this driver listed only [11:8] (TX
   per-lane reset) and [7:4] (TX per-lane enable), and
   `phy-allwinner-a733-add-serdes-combophy`'s header flagged bit0 as a
   vendor-derived name with no doc anchor. That gap is now closed from the spec:
   UM 18.2.6.24 names every bit individually --
   TX_PHY_CFG_LN3..LN0_RSTN = bits 11..8, TX_PHY_CFG_LN3..LN0_EN = bits 7..4,
   TX_PHY_CFG_DATA_EN = bit0, all plain enable bits (0 = disable, 1 = enable) -- and marks 31:12
   unimplemented. So 0xFF1 is "all four lanes' transmitters enabled and out of
   reset, data-width conversion on", every field named by the UM, and an absolute
   write discards nothing.

5. LINK_CTRL3 0x100C bit0 PLLCLK_EN, then poll LINK_STUS0 bit4 CLOCK_EN_ACK,
   budget 5 ms (vendor: 1000 x 5 us).
   Accounts for: bit0 of LINK_CTRL3 = 0x00000011, and bit4 of LINK_STUS0.
   UM 18.2.6.8 bit0 gates the PMA's data-rate clock for every lane of the link,
   so that clock can be stopped cleanly. UM 18.2.6.9 bit4 is its
   acknowledgement and reports whether that clock is running.

6. LINK_CTRL3 [9:4] XCVR_POWER_STATE_REQ = 0x1 (A0), then poll LINK_STUS0 [13:8]
   POWER_STATE_ACK != 0, budget 500 ms (vendor: 100000 x 5 us).
   Accounts for: bits [9:4] of LINK_CTRL3 = 0x00000011 (0x1 << 4 = 0x10, plus
   bit0 from step 5 = 0x11), and bits [13:8] of LINK_STUS0 = 0x00000111
   (0x1 << 8 = 0x100, plus bit4 and bit0 = 0x111).
   UM 18.2.6.8: the field is one-hot, 0x1 = state A0 (Tx/Rx active), and the first
   request after reset release has to be A0 or A2 -- so this step is mandatory, not
   optional tuning. It is written as a masked field, not OR-ed in, because a stale
   bit in a one-hot encoding would request a second state at the same time.
   The budget is 100x the USB path's (PMA_READY_TIMEOUT_US = 10 ms) and that is
   kept, not trimmed: shrinking a vendor poll we cannot reproduce on a bench turns
   a slow-but-working bring-up into a spurious error. Why the DP link needs so
   much longer is not established here and is not claimed.

7. Teardown, on the way back to TYPEC_STATE_USB / TYPEC_STATE_SAFE: LINK_CTRL0 =
   0, DP_RAWS_TXDW_CONV_CTRL = 0, LINK_CTRL3 = 0, in that order. Returning the
   power-state request to zero is not housekeeping: UM 18.2.6.8 says
   pma_xcvr_power_state_req has to be acknowledged and set back to 0 before
   the next state is requested, so leaving A0 standing would
   block the next entry.

Two registers the sequence deliberately does NOT touch: LINK_CTRL1 0x1004, which
reads the same 0x01100001 in the DP capture as it does with SuperSpeed up, so the
refclk routing `phy-allwinner-a733-add-serdes-combophy` programs is personality-independent; and the PMA body, which
the vendor configures through combo0_configure_usb_dp() -- the function `phy-allwinner-a733-add-serdes-combophy`
already ports in full, for both personalities at once, which is why this patch
requires phy_init() to have run and says so in the log if it has not.

USB3 IS NOT TOUCHED
-------------------

DP and USB3 are mutually exclusive on COMB0, and the 10 Gbps link is HW-proven, so
every line added here is behind a DP entry:

  - a733_combophy_dp_reset_seq() and a733_combophy_dp_teardown() are new functions
    with no caller on the USB path. a733_combophy_reset_seq(), the USB sequence,
    is not edited at all -- not one byte of it changes.
  - The only entry is the Type-C mux .set callback, and only for the four-lane,
    DP-only pin assignments C and E. Assignments A, B, D and F keep two lanes on
    USB3; the vendor's path for D/F tears the link down, rewrites the entire PMA
    config and rebuilds it, there is no capture of that state, and it is refused
    with a warning rather than guessed.
  - A new bool dp_active gates the teardown, so the TYPEC_STATE_USB /
    TYPEC_STATE_SAFE transitions that every plug and unplug produces do nothing at
    all on a board that never entered DP.

One existing function does change, and it is worth being explicit about: the
orientation switch callback now re-runs the DP sequence when dp_active is set.
a733_combophy_param_config() updates TYPEC_CONN_DIR with a plain read-modify-write,
which is exactly what UM 18.2.6.5 forbids while phy0_reset_n is de-asserted, so a
direction change under a live DP link has to go back through the staircase. The
call is guarded by dp_active, so a USB-only port never reaches it. In the normal
TCPM flow orientation arrives at attach, before alt-mode entry, so this is a
correctness guard rather than a hot path.

Cost when DP is never entered: one bool in the private struct, and one branch in a
callback that already existed. No change to probe, phy_ops.init, phy_ops.exit, the
PMA tables, or the USB reset sequence.

HOW TO TEST IT -- ONE OBSERVABLE PER STEP
-----------------------------------------

No board was available for this patch; it is written to be testable, and the
kernel's own print is explicitly not the evidence. Plug a DP monitor into J4 on a
board running this tree, then read the wrapper page back with devmem2 (this tree
has no /sys/class/sunxi_dump -- that is a BSP interface):

  step 2   devmem2 0x06C01000 -> 0x00001131 flipped plug / 0x00000131 normal.
           The bit to look at is bit5: it is 0 in the USB-only state and must be 1
           here. bit12 must match the plug orientation reported by
           /sys/class/typec/port0/orientation.
  step 3   devmem2 0x06C01900 bit0 = 1 (also covered by step 6's value).
  step 4   devmem2 0x06C06004 -> 0x00000FF1.
  step 5   devmem2 0x06C0100C bit0 = 1; 0x06C01900 bit4 = 1.
  step 6   devmem2 0x06C0100C -> 0x00000011; 0x06C01900 -> 0x00000111.
  step 7   unplug, then all three of 0x06C01000, 0x06C0100C, 0x06C06004 read 0.

A failing handshake names itself: each of the three polls logs at dev_err with the
raw LINK_STUS0, and the sequence continues instead of returning early, so a single
run says which of the three stalled rather than only the first. The driver also
prints all four registers in one dev_info line at the end -- that line is for
diffing against the capture in a report, not for proving the patch; the kernel is
the thing under test.

Negative control, and the one that actually matters: with no DP monitor attached,
none of the above may change. LINK_CTRL0 must stay 0x00000111 and 0x06C0100C must
stay whatever the USB bring-up left, and `lsusb -t` must still show a SuperSpeed
device at 10000M.

LIMITS
------

Matching a working board's end state is a witness argument, not a mechanism proof
-- the same caveat `phy-allwinner-a733-add-serdes-combophy` carries. It says what the wrapper looks like under a live
link; it does not say that this sequence is what makes the link train.

This patch alone is not expected to produce video, and the driver comment says so
in the same words. Three things are still missing and are named rather than left
implied, each with the capture value that shows the vendor writes them:

  - DP lane map, COMB0_PHY_RAWS_TXD_MAP_CTRL 0x06C06C24: capture 0x00003210,
    reset default 0xFFFF.
  - DP AUX/HPD analog front-end, AUX_HPD_PHY_PINS_CTRL0 0x06C01E00: capture
    0x03331131 (and 0x06C01E04 = 0x00010E0D), documented default 0x00330000. That is a different block with its own driver
    question, and it is what carries AUX and HPD.
  - the PMA link-rate registers the vendor writes in combo0_dp_set_rate(), plus
    lane-count and swing/pre-emphasis. Those live in the PMA body; no
    register documentation for it was available, and they need their own capture.

Pin assignments A, B, D and F are refused, not attempted. A DP entry currently
also ends SuperSpeed for good until phy_exit()/phy_init() run again, because
nothing re-inits the PHY after a DP session; the teardown logs that plainly rather
than pretending otherwise. Restoring the USB personality on DP exit is a separate,
testable change and is deliberately not smuggled in here.

Compile-tested against 6.18.36 with the series applied, W=1, no warnings;
checkpatch --strict clean apart from the "missing commit description" note this
header is.

Assisted-by: Claude

diff --git a/drivers/phy/allwinner/phy-sun60i-a733-combophy.c b/drivers/phy/allwinner/phy-sun60i-a733-combophy.c
--- a/drivers/phy/allwinner/phy-sun60i-a733-combophy.c
+++ b/drivers/phy/allwinner/phy-sun60i-a733-combophy.c
@@ -45,6 +45,15 @@
 #define  DP_PHY_CFG_DATA_EN		BIT(0)
 #define  DP_PHY_CFG_LN_EN		GENMASK(7, 4)
 #define  DP_PHY_CFG_LN_RSTN		GENMASK(11, 8)
+/* All four DP lanes converted, enabled and out of reset -- 0x00000FF1, which is
+ * what 0x06C06004 reads on the 8 GiB board under a live 4-lane DP-alt link
+ * -- UM 18.2.6.24 names every bit
+ * of this register individually (TX_PHY_CFG_LN0..3_RSTN = 11:8,
+ * TX_PHY_CFG_LN0..3_EN = 7:4, TX_PHY_CFG_DATA_EN = bit0) and marks 31:12
+ * unimplemented, so writing it absolutely loses nothing.
+ */
+#define  DP_RAWS_TXDW_CONV_4LANE	(DP_PHY_CFG_DATA_EN | DP_PHY_CFG_LN_EN | \
+					 DP_PHY_CFG_LN_RSTN)
 #define USB3P1_PIPE_CLK_MAP_CTRL	0x0100
 #define USB3P1_PIPE_RXD_MAP_CTRL	0x0104
 #define  USB3P1_PIPE_MAP_MASK		GENMASK(3, 0)
@@ -54,8 +63,25 @@
 #define COMB0_LINK_CTRL0		0x0000
 #define  PHY0_TYPEC_CONN_DIR		BIT(12)
 #define  PHY0_DP_LINK_RESET_N_SOFT	BIT(8)
+/* UM 18.2.6.5 bit5 masks the PIPE reset that the USB3P1 controller drives into PHY0
+ * (1 = masked); that reset acts only while bit5 is 0 and bit4 PIPE_LINK_RESET_N_SOFT is 1.
+ *
+ * Setting it makes bit4 the only source of the PIPE link reset and takes the dwc3 core's
+ * line out of the picture -- which is what the lanes need while they carry DP.
+ */
+#define  PHY0_PIPE_LINK_RESET_N_USB3P1_MASK	BIT(5)
 #define  PHY0_PIPE_LINK_RESET_N_SOFT	BIT(4)
 #define  PHY0_RESET_N			BIT(0)
+/* The DP personality releases LINK_CTRL0 as a staircase of absolute values
+ * (0x001 -> 0x101 -> 0x131, plus TYPEC_CONN_DIR), not as read-modify-writes of
+ * single bits the way the USB path does. See a733_combophy_dp_reset_seq().
+ */
+#define  COMB0_LINK_CTRL0_DP_PHY	PHY0_RESET_N
+#define  COMB0_LINK_CTRL0_DP_LINK	(COMB0_LINK_CTRL0_DP_PHY | \
+					 PHY0_DP_LINK_RESET_N_SOFT)
+#define  COMB0_LINK_CTRL0_DP_RUN	(COMB0_LINK_CTRL0_DP_LINK | \
+					 PHY0_PIPE_LINK_RESET_N_SOFT | \
+					 PHY0_PIPE_LINK_RESET_N_USB3P1_MASK)
 #define COMB0_LINK_CTRL1		0x0004
 #define  PHY0_PMA_CMN_PLL1_REFCLK_SEL	BIT(24)
 #define  PHY0_PMA_CMN_PLL0_REFCLK_SEL	BIT(20)
@@ -72,6 +98,11 @@
 #define  PHY0_IDDQ_EN_SOFT		BIT(0)
 #define COMB0_LINK_CTRL3		0x000C
 #define  PHY0_PMA_XCVR_POWER_STATE_REQ_LN(n)	(((n) & 0x3f) << 4)
+#define  PHY0_PMA_XCVR_POWER_STATE_REQ	GENMASK(9, 4)
+/* UM 18.2.6.8: [9:4] is one-hot, 0x1 = A0 (Tx/Rx active); the first request
+ * after reset release has to be A0 or A2.
+ */
+#define  PHY0_PMA_XCVR_POWER_STATE_A0	PHY0_PMA_XCVR_POWER_STATE_REQ_LN(1)
 #define  PHY0_PMA_XCVR_PLLCLK_EN_LN	BIT(0)
 #define COMB0_LINK_STUS0		0x0900
 #define  PHY0_PMA_CMN_READY		BIT(0)
@@ -91,6 +122,20 @@
 #define PHY_LOCK_TIMEOUT_US		2000
 #define PMA_READY_TIMEOUT_US		10000
 
+/* DP bring-up poll budgets, kept at the vendor's values rather than shrunk to
+ * the USB ones: combo0_dp_phy_reset() allows 1000 x 5 us for PMA_CMN_READY and
+ * for CLOCK_EN_ACK, and 100000 x 5 us -- half a second -- for POWER_STATE_ACK.
+ * A shorter poll would turn a slow-but-working bring-up into a spurious error.
+ */
+#define DP_POLL_STEP_US			5
+#define DP_ACK_TIMEOUT_US		5000
+#define DP_POWER_STATE_TIMEOUT_US	500000
+/* Vendor uses msleep(1) between the LINK_CTRL0 steps; usleep_range() is the
+ * in-tree spelling for a sub-20 ms sleep and guarantees at least as long.
+ */
+#define DP_RESET_STEP_MIN_US		1000
+#define DP_RESET_STEP_MAX_US		2000
+
 struct a733_combophy {
 	struct device		*dev;
 	void __iomem		*link;	/* combophy0 top   0x06c01000 */
@@ -117,6 +162,11 @@
 	 */
 	struct typec_mux_dev	*mux;
 	unsigned long		mux_mode;
+	/* True between a DP pin-assignment entry and the return to USB/SAFE.
+	 * It is the only thing that makes the DP register writes reachable, so
+	 * a board that never enters DP alt mode never executes any of them.
+	 */
+	bool			dp_active;
 	bool			inited;
 };
 
@@ -560,6 +610,130 @@
 	return 0;
 }
 
+/*
+ * combo0_dp_phy_reset(deassert = true) -- the DP counterpart of
+ * a733_combophy_reset_seq(). Same registers, materially different sequence, and
+ * every difference is deliberate; see the patch header for the derivation.
+ *
+ * Target end state, all four values measured on the 8 GiB board under a live 4-lane DP-alt
+ * link with the vendor stack:
+ *
+ *	LINK_CTRL0 0x06C01000 = 0x00001131   (0x0131 with a normal plug)
+ *	LINK_CTRL3 0x06C0100C = 0x00000011
+ *	LINK_STUS0 0x06C01900 = 0x00000111   (read-only, the PHY's answer)
+ *	DP_RAWS_TXDW 0x06C06004 = 0x00000FF1
+ *
+ * Failures are logged per step and collected, not returned early: the vendor
+ * warns and carries on, and carrying on is what tells a tester which of the
+ * three handshakes stalled instead of only the first.
+ */
+static int a733_combophy_dp_reset_seq(struct a733_combophy *cp)
+{
+	u32 dir = cp->orientation == TYPEC_ORIENTATION_REVERSE ?
+		  PHY0_TYPEC_CONN_DIR : 0;
+	u32 run = dir | COMB0_LINK_CTRL0_DP_RUN;
+	int ret, err = 0;
+	u32 v;
+
+	/* LINK_CTRL2 0x1008: out of IDDQ. Idempotent -- the USB bring-up already
+	 * did this, and 0x06C01008 reads 0x00000000 in the DP capture.
+	 */
+	a733_combophy_power_up(cp);
+
+	/*
+	 * LINK_CTRL0 0x1000, absolute values. TYPEC_CONN_DIR rides in every one
+	 * of them because UM 18.2.6.5 requires bit12 to stay static
+	 * while phy0_reset_n is de-asserted. The vendor writes the direction
+	 * after the last step, i.e. after phy0_reset_n is already high; folding
+	 * it into the staircase honours the UM instead and reaches the identical
+	 * end value. If a board ever disagrees, the vendor's literal ordering is
+	 * one line away.
+	 */
+	writel(dir, cp->link + COMB0_LINK_CTRL0);
+	writel(dir | COMB0_LINK_CTRL0_DP_PHY, cp->link + COMB0_LINK_CTRL0);
+	usleep_range(DP_RESET_STEP_MIN_US, DP_RESET_STEP_MAX_US);
+	writel(dir | COMB0_LINK_CTRL0_DP_LINK, cp->link + COMB0_LINK_CTRL0);
+	usleep_range(DP_RESET_STEP_MIN_US, DP_RESET_STEP_MAX_US);
+	writel(run, cp->link + COMB0_LINK_CTRL0);
+	usleep_range(DP_RESET_STEP_MIN_US, DP_RESET_STEP_MAX_US);
+	/* the vendor's fourth millisecond, spent on its separate direction
+	 * write; kept as settling time so the total is unchanged.
+	 */
+	usleep_range(DP_RESET_STEP_MIN_US, DP_RESET_STEP_MAX_US);
+
+	/* LINK_STUS0 0x1900 bit0, PMA common ready (UM 18.2.6.9), which the UM
+	 * ties to the Raw SerDes startup and to power-state changes --
+	 * so it is waited for BEFORE the raw-serdes transmitters are enabled,
+	 * which is the other way round from the USB path.
+	 */
+	ret = readl_poll_timeout(cp->link + COMB0_LINK_STUS0, v,
+				 v & PHY0_PMA_CMN_READY,
+				 DP_POLL_STEP_US, DP_ACK_TIMEOUT_US);
+	if (ret) {
+		dev_err(cp->dev, "DP: PMA_CMN_READY never set, LINK_STUS0 = 0x%08x\n",
+			v);
+		err = ret;
+	}
+
+	/* DP_RAWS_TXDW_CONV_CTRL 0x6004 -> 0x0FF1 */
+	writel(DP_RAWS_TXDW_CONV_4LANE, cp->top2 + DP_RAWS_TXDW_CONV_CTRL);
+
+	/* LINK_CTRL3 0x100c bit0 PLLCLK_EN, answered in LINK_STUS0 bit4 */
+	a733_rmw(cp->link, COMB0_LINK_CTRL3, 0, PHY0_PMA_XCVR_PLLCLK_EN_LN);
+	ret = readl_poll_timeout(cp->link + COMB0_LINK_STUS0, v,
+				 v & PHY0_PMA_XCVR_CLK_EN_ACK,
+				 DP_POLL_STEP_US, DP_ACK_TIMEOUT_US);
+	if (ret) {
+		dev_err(cp->dev, "DP: CLOCK_EN_ACK never set, LINK_STUS0 = 0x%08x\n",
+			v);
+		err = ret;
+	}
+
+	usleep_range(DP_RESET_STEP_MIN_US, DP_RESET_STEP_MAX_US);
+
+	/* LINK_CTRL3 [9:4] = A0. Written as a masked field, not OR-ed in: the
+	 * encoding is one-hot, so a stale bit would request a second state.
+	 */
+	a733_rmw(cp->link, COMB0_LINK_CTRL3, PHY0_PMA_XCVR_POWER_STATE_REQ,
+		 PHY0_PMA_XCVR_POWER_STATE_A0);
+	ret = readl_poll_timeout(cp->link + COMB0_LINK_STUS0, v,
+				 v & PHY0_PMA_XCVR_POWER_STATE_ACK,
+				 DP_POLL_STEP_US, DP_POWER_STATE_TIMEOUT_US);
+	if (ret) {
+		dev_err(cp->dev, "DP: POWER_STATE_ACK never set, LINK_STUS0 = 0x%08x\n",
+			v);
+		err = ret;
+	}
+
+	/* One line a tester can diff straight against the 8 GiB board's capture. It stays
+	 * at info level until the sequence has silicon behind it.
+	 */
+	dev_info(cp->dev,
+		 "DP: LINK_CTRL0 0x%08x LINK_CTRL3 0x%08x LINK_STUS0 0x%08x DP_RAWS_TXDW 0x%08x (want 0x%08x / 0x00000011 / 0x00000111 / 0x00000ff1)\n",
+		 readl(cp->link + COMB0_LINK_CTRL0),
+		 readl(cp->link + COMB0_LINK_CTRL3),
+		 readl(cp->link + COMB0_LINK_STUS0),
